The Solar System has the Sun at its center, which provides light and heat.
It contains 8 planets orbiting the Sun, from Mercury to Neptune.
Moons, asteroids, and comets also orbit the Sun alongside planets.
The planets are divided into terrestrial (rocky) and gas giants.
The Solar System is part of the Milky Way galaxy.
The asteroid belt lies between Mars and Jupiter, full of rocky bodies.
Comets are icy bodies that develop glowing tails when near the Sun.
The Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud are distant regions with icy objects at the edge of the Solar System.
The planets orbit the Sun in elliptical paths due to gravity.
Gravity from the Sun keeps all planets, moons, and objects in orbit.
Some planets, like Jupiter and Saturn, have ring systems made of ice and rock.
